\details{
\strong{Inputs.} For each sample, \verb{<prefix>_subclones.txt} is read along
with, when present in the same directory, \verb{<prefix>_cellularity_ploidy.txt},
\verb{<prefix>_rho_and_psi.txt} and \verb{<prefix>_copynumber_solution_status.txt}.
The sample name is the file prefix. Only autosomes are used (sex
chromosomes depend on sex). Chromosome names are compared without a "chr"
prefix.

\strong{Copy number states.} Each segment's state is the clonal state
(\code{nMaj1_A}, \code{nMin1_A}) or, for subclonal segments, whichever of the two
states covers more cells. Segments with \code{frac1_A < 1} count as subclonal.
The sample's baseline is its modal total copy number (by length).

\strong{LOH classes} (minor allele = 0, total > 0), relative to the
baseline: \code{loh_neutral} (total = baseline; e.g. 2+0 in a diploid genome,
4+0 in a tetraploid one), \code{loh_loss} (total below baseline; e.g. 1+0 in a
diploid genome, 2+0 in a tetraploid one) and \code{loh_gain} (total above
baseline). Total copy number 0 is a homozygous deletion. Segments of the
same class are merged into events, including across interruptions of up to
\code{merge_max_gap} (e.g. a short segment of another state inside a large LOH
region), before counting events and measuring their sizes; genome fractions
use all segments.

\strong{Terminal events} reach the first or last called position of their
chromosome; the others are interstitial (counted in
\code{loh_neutral_interstitial_events} and \code{loh_loss_interstitial_events}, for
events of at least \code{min_event_size}). Copy-neutral LOH from mitotic recombination is typically
terminal, whereas LOH from deletion is often interstitial.

\strong{Whole-genome doubling} (\code{doubled}) is a heuristic, not an
evolutionary inference: TRUE when at least half of the autosomal genome has
a major allele copy number of 2 or more. It is distinct from the average
ploidy (\code{ploidy}, from Battenberg; \code{mean_total_cn}, from the calls) and from
the modal baseline (\code{modal_cn}, \code{baseline_cn}) used to call relative gains
and losses. In doubled
genomes, LOH with an even major allele copy number (2+0, 4+0) is
consistent with LOH before doubling (a single-copy or copy-neutral LOH that
was then doubled), and an odd one (1+0, 3+0) with loss after doubling.

\strong{Shares.} \code{loh_neutral_share} is the copy-neutral fraction of LOH,
\code{loh_neutral_fraction / (loh_neutral_fraction + loh_loss_fraction)}.
\code{loss_fraction} and \code{gain_fraction} are the genome below and above the
baseline (including homozygous deletions in the former), and
\code{loss_with_loh_share} is the share of that lost genome with LOH,
\code{loh_loss_fraction / loss_fraction}. All use the same calls and baseline;
they are NA when the denominator is 0.

\strong{Event counts and small events.} Event counts and sizes include
only merged events of at least \code{min_event_size}; genome fractions include
every segment. A sample can therefore have a small nonzero LOH fraction
with zero counted events (\code{loh_neutral_small_events} and
\code{loh_loss_small_events} count the events below the threshold).

\strong{Allele-specific properties.} Three properties are recorded for
each segment independently, in \code{segments}: \code{cn_change} (total copy number
below, at or above the baseline: loss, neutral, gain), \code{homologues} (both
parental homologues present, i.e. minor allele > 0; one, i.e. complete
LOH; none, i.e. total 0) and \code{balance} (for segments with both homologues:
balanced, major = minor, or imbalanced). For example, at a baseline of 4,
1+1 is a loss retaining both homologues (balanced), 2+0 a loss with
complete LOH, 2+1 a loss retaining both (imbalanced), 3+1 copy-neutral
imbalance and 4+0 copy-neutral complete LOH. The allele copy numbers and
the \code{allelic_state} label (e.g. "2+1") are kept. Allele copy numbers must
be integers (as Battenberg reports them); other values are unknown, not
rounded. For a subclonal segment, each property comes from the dominant
state when it covers at least \code{min_clonal_fraction} of tumour cells,
otherwise only if both states agree; when they disagree the property is
NA. The baseline for these properties, \code{baseline_cn}, is the modal total
copy number over segments whose total is known; it normally equals
\code{modal_cn} (which uses each segment's majority state).

\strong{Allele-specific fractions.} Their denominator is the assessable
genome: autosomal segments whose copy number change, homologues and (for
segments with both homologues) balance are all known
(\code{assessable_genome_mb}; \code{assessable_fraction} is its share of the called
genome, \code{called_genome_mb}, and \code{coverage_fraction} the called share of
the reference autosomes, \code{reference_autosome_mb}, excluding
centromeres). Unassessable or uncalled regions are not counted as
heterozygous or copy-neutral, and these fractions are NA when nothing is
assessable. Fractions of the assessable genome: \code{complete_loh_fraction}
(one homologue) and its split by copy number change,
\code{complete_loh_loss/neutral/gain_fraction}; \code{relative_loss_fraction};
\code{neutral_fraction}; \code{no_homologue_fraction} (total 0).
\code{loh_copy_neutral_share} is the copy-neutral share of complete LOH
(including LOH with gain). \code{allelic_states} gives the genome in each
allele-specific state per sample (e.g. 2+2 vs 4+0), from which other
combinations can be derived; it covers segments whose exact state is known,
so its fractions (of the assessable genome) can sum to slightly less than 1.
\code{mean_total_cn} is the length-weighted mean total copy number over segments
whose exact total is known.

These measure detectable changes in parental allele composition only. A
repair or recombination event that copies one homologue from another copy
of the same parental homologue (e.g. a sister chromatid, or after
doubling, the other copy of that parent: AA'BB' to AA'B'B') leaves the
allele state unchanged (2+2) and is invisible here. They are endpoint
states, not rates of loss or recombination.

\strong{Fitting stages and QC.} From \verb{<prefix>_rho_and_psi.txt}, by row:
\code{ascat_purity}/\code{ascat_ploidy} (initial ASCAT fit), \code{clonal_fit_purity},
\code{clonal_fit_ploidy} and \code{clonal_fit_score} (the FRAC_GENOME clonal refit,
which the final profile is fitted from, and its distance) and
\code{ref_seg_ploidy}; \code{final_profile_ploidy} is the ploidy in
\verb{<prefix>_cellularity_ploidy.txt} (the same as \code{ploidy}). The
\verb{ploidy_delta_*} columns are signed differences (later stage minus earlier).
\code{fit_distance} is unchanged: the distance of the row marked \code{is.best}.
\code{refit_suggestions} counts the rows of \verb{<prefix>_refit_suggestion.txt} (NA
if absent). Missing or malformed files leave these NA. QC annotation
(\code{allelic_qc_status}, \code{allelic_qc_reasons} and \verb{qc_*} flags) is added by
\link{battenberg_qc}.

\strong{Homozygous deletion size.} \code{homdel_mb} is the total size of
homozygous deletion (total copy number 0) and \code{largest_homdel_mb} the
largest run of consecutive homozygously deleted segments (deleted sequence
only; unlike \code{loh_segments} events, not merged across gaps).
Homozygous deletions of tens of Mb are not compatible with a viable cell
and usually point to a misfit solution; \link{battenberg_qc} flags them.

\strong{Excluded LOH.} \code{excluded_loh_fraction} is the share of the called
genome that is LOH by majority state but not assessable (e.g. subclonal
segments whose states disagree). A low \code{complete_loh_fraction} with a high
\code{excluded_loh_fraction} means LOH was excluded, not absent; the original
\verb{loh_*} columns report LOH by majority state over the called genome.

\strong{Complete LOH events} (\code{complete_loh_events}, and the \verb{cnloh_*}
summary columns). Built from the allele-specific properties (assessable
segments with one homologue and an exact total copy number), so
unresolved subclonal segments are left out; consecutive segments with the
same total copy number form an event. Unlike \code{loh_segments} (merged by
majority state over the called genome), these are allele-specific.
Each event's \code{loh_type} (neutral, loss or gain) is relative to
\code{cnloh_reference}. What counts as copy-neutral is
set by \code{cnloh_reference}:
\itemize{
\item "sample" (default): total copy number equals the sample's baseline
(\code{baseline_cn}), e.g. 2+0 in a diploid genome and 4+0 in a doubled one.
\item "diploid": total copy number 2 whatever the baseline, e.g. 2+0 in a
doubled genome too (where 4+0 is then LOH with gain).
}
In a doubled genome, 4+0 is most simply copy-neutral LOH before doubling
(2+0, then doubled), while 2+0 is most simply a single-copy loss before
doubling (1+0, then doubled); a single copy-neutral event after doubling
gives 3+1, not LOH. The event table holds every complete LOH event, with
\code{neutral_vs_sample} and \code{neutral_vs_diploid} so either definition can be
applied without re-running; \code{large} marks events of at least
\code{min_event_size} and \code{counted} the large copy-neutral ones under the
chosen reference (those in the \verb{cnloh_*} counts).
Events are merged across uncalled sequence (e.g. centromeres) and across
up to \code{merge_max_gap} of segments in other states.
Each event's \code{extent}, measured against the called part of its chromosome
(uncalled regions, e.g. near centromeres, don't count against it), is
\code{whole_chromosome} (at least \code{arm_fraction} of the chromosome),
\code{arm} (at least \code{arm_fraction} of an arm), \code{terminal} (reaching the first
or last called position) or \code{interstitial}. These extents suggest
different mechanisms (e.g. missegregation with reduplication for whole
chromosomes, mitotic recombination for terminal events), so whole-
chromosome events are reported separately: \code{cnloh_fraction} includes them
and \code{cnloh_segmental_fraction} excludes them (both of the assessable
genome, from all segments); counts and Mb per extent use the counted
events. Two copy-neutral shares of LOH leave out whole-chromosome LOH of
any copy number (e.g. monosomy as well as whole-chromosome copy-neutral
LOH), from both numerator and denominator:
\code{loh_copy_neutral_share_segmental} (by length: copy-neutral share of
complete LOH with an exact total copy number) and
\code{loh_copy_neutral_event_share} (by events of at least \code{min_event_size},
each event counting once, so not dominated by a few large events); NA when
there is no such LOH. Both follow \code{cnloh_reference}, unlike
\code{loh_copy_neutral_share}, which is always relative to the sample's
baseline and includes whole-chromosome events. Also per event: \code{subclonal_fraction} (share in subclonal
segments), \code{baf_deviation} (mean |observed - expected| major-allele BAF at
the fitted purity; large values suggest a poor fit) and the \code{loh_genes} it
covers. The older \verb{loh_neutral_*} columns use majority states over the
called genome and the sample baseline.

\strong{Allelic imbalance without LOH} (minor allele > 0, major > minor) is
split the same way: \code{imbalance_gain} (e.g. 2+1 in a diploid genome),
\code{imbalance_neutral} (e.g. 3+1 in a tetraploid one) and \code{imbalance_loss}.
}
